Z||000000||four50four||11-12-1999||05:46 PM||||is there any way to get a e-brake to work?||||reg||4
Z||000001||Tim||11-12-1999||06:40 PM||||Add a small mechanical caliper from a dirt bike.||||reg||1
Z||000002||four50four||11-12-1999||06:55 PM||||what kind of dirt bike? wouldn't the caliper be way to small, and aren't most of them hydraulic?||||reg||6
Z||000003||Tim||11-12-1999||07:36 PM||||You would not use it to stop the car, only to keep it from moving.  Some states require brakes.  As for what bikes ask a dirt rider.  You would have to make mounts and levers and thin the pads.  I would have left the stock calipers, less unsprung weight.||||reg||1
Z||000004||four50four||11-12-1999||07:48 PM||||i don't see how a dirt bike caliper will fit. i own numerous dirt bikes, and the discs are very thin, compared to a car. no way a bike caliper would fit||||reg||1
Z||000005||Tim||11-12-1999||08:00 PM||||I'm sorry, I'm thinking of the two piece caliper, add a spacer between the halves or make the mount part of the spacer.  I remember dirt bikes in the 70's when they first started coming with disk brakes.||||reg||1
Z||000006||TOM||11-12-1999||08:19 PM||||Some toyotas have a small mechanical rear e brake calpier||||reg||1
Z||000007||HarryG||11-18-1999||12:17 PM||||Wilwood makes a "Mechanical Spot Caliper" which looks like it could be adapted to work<BR>with these rotors.  You'd have to make a spacer to fit between the caliper halves plus a mounting bracket.  I've seen several mentions of this, but they all seemed to be in the idea stage. Haven't heard of anyone actually doing it.   ||||reg||3
Z||000008||WKDFIRO||11-18-1999||03:41 PM||||I know someone who just finished putting the Grand Am brakes on and they had a fully functional e brake. I think they mentioned that they had to fabricate a bracket or something. I will find out and report. I seem to remember the driver side caliper had to be put on upside down for some reason.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000009||fastonpumpgas||11-19-1999||12:06 AM||||I dont understand why the stock replacement ebrake capliper doesnt work?||||reg||1
Z||000010||four50four||11-19-1999||06:59 AM||||when doing the brake upgrade, you have to use front rotors and calipers, due to the fact that grand ams have drum brakes in the rear. you end up with front brakes on the rear of your fiero, so there is no e-brake||||reg||2
Z||000011||fierosound||11-19-1999||12:18 PM||||I've been checking around on the net and found that Grand Am GT models come with power 4-wheel disc brakes.  I'm not sure what year they were introduced, but wouldn't it make more sense to use these calipers for the rear brake conversion?  Anyone know if these would fit?<P>Grand AM features (see GT model)  <A HREF="http://www.pontiac.com/grandam/features.html" TARGET=_blank>www.pontiac.com/grandam/features.html</A> <P>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 11-19-1999).]<p>[This message has been edited by fierosound (edited 11-19-1999).]||||reg||3
Z||000012||batboy||11-19-1999||04:43 PM||||The Grand Am brake swap they are referring to (and what I've done to the Batmobile), uses calipers and rotors from the mid 80's to 1990 Grand Am. I believe the GT models you mentioned with the 4 wheel discs are newer than that.||||reg||1
Z||000013||RobH||11-19-1999||06:28 PM||||batboy --<P>Did you find the brake conversion worth it?  I am seriously thinking about doing that on mine.  Did you do the Blazer Master Cylinder replacement too?  Any comments appreciated.<P>Thanks...<BR>Rob||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||11-19-1999||08:48 PM||||In my case, yes it was worth it. The Batmobile has a big engine and goes fast, so I needed better brakes, plus the old brakes were about shot. The car now brakes a little better than stock, not a big difference until you are high performance driving, then they dont't fade nearly as bad because of the vented rotors. The caliper pistons are a bit bigger for slightly more clamping power, but the rotors are actually the same diameter as the Fiero (but thicker and vented). The cost is about the same as an entire Fiero brake rebuild. No, I haven't changed the master cylinder yet, but I might this spring just because that's the only thing that's not new yet. However, you do loose the parking brake function. If your brakes are in great shape and you don't do much hotrodding, it's probably a waste of money for you.||||reg||1
